[{"_1":2},"llms/mdx",{"_3":4},"data","# Configuration reference (/docs/pr-preview/pr-56/platform/updates/reference/configuration)\n\n\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-ins lm-diff-block\">\n  Lamassu Updates reads a single YAML file at start-up. This page lists every key the code reads, its default, and which backend (`native` or `hawkbit`) uses it. Keys that appear in the example files but are not read are listed separately so you do not rely on them.\n</div>\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-ins lm-diff-block\">\n  How the configuration is loaded [#how-the-configuration-is-loaded]\n</div>\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-ins lm-diff-block\">\n  * Set the file location with the `OTA_CONFIG_FILE` environment variable. If it is unset, the service reads `/etc/lamassuiot/config.yml`, which is where the Docker image and the Helm chart mount it.\n  * If `OTA_CONFIG_FILE` names a file that cannot be loaded (missing, or invalid YAML), the service logs a warning and **falls back to `/etc/lamassuiot/config.yml`**. In the Docker image that path holds the repository's developer `config.yml`, so a typo in the path can start the service with the wrong settings. If the fallback fails too, the service exits.\n  * The file is read once. Restart the service after any change.\n  * `LAMASSU_CONFIG_FILE`, which the Dockerfile and the Helm chart set, is not read by the code. Only `OTA_CONFIG_FILE` matters.\n  * Any key can be overridden by an environment variable. See [Environment variables](#environment-variables).\n</div>\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-ins lm-diff-block\">\n  The [repository](https://github.com/lamassuiot/ota) ships three examples:\n</div>\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-ins lm-diff-block\">\n  | File                 | For                                                                                          |\n  | -------------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n  | `config.yml`         | Local development in `native` mode                                                           |\n  | `config-docker.yml`  | The `updates` container of `docker-compose.yml`                                              |\n  | `config-hawkbit.yml` | Local development in `hawkbit` mode against the hawkBit of the compose file (host port 8090) |\n</div>\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-ins lm-diff-block\">\n  The examples contain values for one developer machine (absolute paths and LAN addresses) and development secrets. Replace all of them, and never reuse their keys or passwords.\n</div>\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-ins lm-diff-block\">\n  Which keys each mode reads [#which-keys-each-mode-reads]\n</div>\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-ins lm-diff-block\">\n  | Key                                                     | `native`       | `hawkbit`                  |\n  | ------------------------------------------------------- | -------------- | -------------------------- |\n  | `mode`, `app.*`, `storage.*`                            | Yes            | Yes                        |\n  | `dms_endpoint`                                          | Yes (required) | Optional                   |\n  | `kms_endpoint`, `sign_script_path`, `swugenerator_path` | Yes            | Yes, only to build or sign |\n  | `symkms_endpoint`, `symkms`, `sym_keys_dir`             | Yes            | Yes, only for encryption   |\n  | `max_artifact_size_mb`, `enable_test_endpoints`         | Yes            | Yes                        |\n  | `wfx_endpoint`, `workflow_file`, `update_domain`        | Yes            | No                         |\n  | `update_files_base_dir`, `artifacts`, `builds`          | Yes            | No                         |\n  | `leader_election`                                       | Yes            | No                         |\n  | `hawkbit`                                               | No             | Yes (required)             |\n  | `build_memory_limit_mb`                                 | No             | Yes                        |\n</div>\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-ins lm-diff-block\">\n  `mode` is `native` when empty or when it holds any value other than `hawkbit`.\n</div>\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-ins lm-diff-block\">\n  General and server settings [#general-and-server-settings]\n</div>\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-ins lm-diff-block\">\n  | Key                           | Type     | Default                | Description                                                                                                                                                                                                                      |\n  | ----------------------------- | -------- | ---------------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n  | `mode`                        | string   | empty (`native`)       | `native` or `hawkbit`. See [hawkBit mode](/docs/platform/updates/hawkbit-mode).                                                                                                                                                  |\n  | `app.logs.level`              | string   | `info`                 | `trace`, `debug`, `info`, `warn`, `error`, `fatal` or `none` (case-insensitive). An unrecognised value becomes `info`. At `info` the start-up log prints a harmless \"unknown log level\" warning. `none` discards all log output. |\n  | `app.server.listen_address`   | string   | empty (all interfaces) | Address to bind. The examples use `0.0.0.0`.                                                                                                                                                                                     |\n  | `app.server.port`             | integer  | none (required)        | HTTP port. `0` picks a random port. The examples and the chart use `10090`.                                                                                                                                                      |\n  | `app.server.shutdown_timeout` | duration | `60s`                  | How long to wait for in-flight requests after `SIGTERM` or `SIGINT`. Keep it below the termination grace period of the pod (the chart sets 120 s).                                                                               |\n  | `enable_test_endpoints`       | boolean  | `false`                | Registers `PUT /updates/v1/jobs/:jobID/transition`, which forges a job state change without ownership checks. Use only in tests; keep it `false` anywhere else.                                                                  |\n</div>\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-ins lm-diff-block\">\n  The service always serves plain HTTP. These keys exist in the configuration structs and in the example files but **are not read**, so setting them has no effect: `app.server.protocol`, `app.server.log_level`, `app.server.health_check` (and the `health_check_logging` of the examples), `app.server.cert_file`, `app.server.key_file` and `app.server.authentication.mutual_tls.*`. Terminate TLS in front of the service. See [Security](/docs/platform/updates/security).\n</div>\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-ins lm-diff-block\">\n  Database [#database]\n</div>\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-ins lm-diff-block\">\n  ```yaml\n  storage:\n    provider: \"postgres\"\n    hostname: \"postgres\"\n    port: 5432\n    username: \"postgres\"\n    password: \"<password>\"\n    log_level: info\n  ```\n</div>\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-ins lm-diff-block\">\n  | Key                                    | Type    | Default         | Description                                                            |\n  | -------------------------------------- | ------- | --------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n  | `storage.provider`                     | string  | none            | Only `postgres` exists. The value is not checked.                      |\n  | `storage.hostname`                     | string  | none (required) | Postgres host. In `hawkbit` mode startup fails if it is empty.         |\n  | `storage.port`                         | integer | none (required) | Postgres port. There is no default.                                    |\n  | `storage.username`, `storage.password` | string  | none            | Credentials.                                                           |\n  | `storage.log_level`                    | string  | `info`          | Log level of the database layer. SQL statements are logged at `trace`. |\n</div>\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-ins lm-diff-block\">\n  * The database name is fixed to **`updates`**. There is no key for it. The service creates its tables and migrates them at start-up, but it does not create the database: create `updates` first. In Docker Compose, `init.sql` creates `updates` and `hawkbit`, and `POSTGRES_DB` creates `wfx`.\n  * The connection uses `sslmode=disable`. There is no way to enable TLS towards Postgres from the configuration. Keep the database on a private network.\n  * Each process keeps at most 25 open and 10 idle connections (connections are recycled after 30 minutes, or 5 idle minutes). Budget `25 x replicas` against the `max_connections` of Postgres.\n</div>\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-ins lm-diff-block\">\n  Endpoints of other services [#endpoints-of-other-services]\n</div>\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-ins lm-diff-block\">\n  | Key               | Default | Mode                                        | Description                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                   |\n  | ----------------- | ------- | ------------------------------------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n  | `dms_endpoint`    | none    | Required in `native`, optional in `hawkbit` | Base URL of the Lamassu Device Manager API, for example `http://host:8080/api/devmanager`. It is the source of device groups. Without it in `hawkbit` mode, groups are read from the `group:<id>` tags of the hawkBit targets. The service sends `X-Principal-ID: admin-mode` on these calls. |\n  | `kms_endpoint`    | none    | Both                                        | Base URL of the Lamassu KMS API, for example `http://host:8080/api/kms/v1`. Needed to sign. See [Signing and encryption](/docs/platform/updates/signing-and-encryption).                                                                                                                      |\n  | `symkms_endpoint` | none    | Both                                        | Base URL of SymKMS, **without** the `/symkms/v1` path. In the monolith it points at the service itself (for example `http://localhost:10090`) and must match `app.server.port`. Empty means encryption is refused.                                                                            |\n  | `wfx_endpoint`    | none    | `native`                                    | Base URL of the WFX **management** API (port `8081` inside the Compose network, `9081` on the host). The service appends `/api/wfx/v1/`. Startup fails if WFX cannot be reached. Devices use the WFX client port instead.                                                                     |\n  | `update_domain`   | none    | `native`                                    | Public base URL devices use to download builds, for example `http://updates:10090/updates/v1/`. It **must end in `/updates/v1/`, with the trailing slash**: the service appends `groups/...` directly and stores the result as the pack URI. It must be reachable from the devices.           |\n</div>\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-ins lm-diff-block\">\n  Build and signing [#build-and-signing]\n</div>\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-ins lm-diff-block\">\n  | Key                     | Default                                                           | Mode      | Description                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                         |\n  | ----------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------- | --------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n  | `swugenerator_path`     | `swugenerator` on the `PATH` in `native`; unset in `hawkbit`      | Both      | Path to the `swugenerator` binary. In `hawkbit` mode the SWU builder exists only when this is set: without it, `swu-build` modules cannot be built (`501`). Raw-module builds do not need it.                                                                       |\n  | `sign_script_path`      | none                                                              | Both      | The signer script swugenerator calls for `.swu` signing, `sign_kms.sh` (`/app/sign_kms.sh` in the image). Signing a `.swu` needs this and `kms_endpoint`; the service refuses a signing request when either is missing.                                             |\n  | `workflow_file`         | `wfx.workflow.dau.direct.yml` (relative to the working directory) | `native`  | Path of the `direct` WFX workflow. The name **must contain `direct`**: the `phased`, `download-install` and `simple` files are found by replacing that word in the path, and startup fails otherwise. The service uploads the workflows missing in WFX at start-up. |\n  | `update_files_base_dir` | none                                                              | `native`  | Base directory for build scratch (`in/`) and, until builds are published to the `builds` store, build outputs (`out/`). It also supplies the default `local_dir` of `artifacts` and `builds`.                                                                       |\n  | `sym_keys_dir`          | none                                                              | Both      | Fallback directory for SymKMS key storage when `symkms.storage.local_dir` is empty.                                                                                                                                                                                 |\n  | `build_memory_limit_mb` | `0` (no check)                                                    | `hawkbit` | Refuses a build that would encrypt a file in memory (about twice its size) above this limit, in MiB, before the process is killed for lack of memory.                                                                                                               |\n</div>\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-ins lm-diff-block\">\n  Upload limits [#upload-limits]\n</div>\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-ins lm-diff-block\">\n  | Key                    | Type    | Default          | Description                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                 |\n  | ---------------------- | ------- | ---------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n  | `max_artifact_size_mb` | integer | `10240` (10 GiB) | Largest single uploaded file in MiB: artifact or prebuilt `.swu`. A larger multipart file gets `413` with the message `file \"<name>\" is <n> MiB; the maximum upload size is <m> MiB`. The HTTP body limit is this value plus 1 MiB. Values of `0` or less mean the default. |\n</div>\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-ins lm-diff-block\">\n  Uploads are streamed: memory does not grow with the file, but each in-flight upload is written to a temporary file under `TMPDIR` (default `/tmp`) first, so it needs that much free disk. Any proxy in front of the service must accept the same size and keep the connection open: read and write timeouts are 6 hours and idle connections are closed after 5 minutes. See [Operations](/docs/platform/updates/operations#upload-size-and-proxies).\n</div>\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-ins lm-diff-block\">\n  Blob storage blocks [#blob-storage-blocks]\n</div>\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-ins lm-diff-block\">\n  Four blocks share one layout, so artifacts, build outputs and key material can each live on local disk or in S3:\n</div>\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-ins lm-diff-block\">\n  | Block                    | Mode      | Holds                                                                                           | Default                                                                              |\n  | ------------------------ | --------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ |\n  | `artifacts`              | `native`  | Uploaded artifact binaries                                                                      | Local, `{update_files_base_dir}/artifacts`                                           |\n  | `builds`                 | `native`  | Build outputs devices download: `.swu` and `.tar.gz`                                            | Local, `{update_files_base_dir}/out`                                                 |\n  | `symkms`                 | Both      | Symmetric keys and device-key assignments                                                       | Local, `sym_keys_dir`, or `data` relative to the working directory if both are empty |\n  | `hawkbit.artifact_store` | `hawkbit` | Read only, to sign download URLs of hawkBit's S3 bucket (see [`hawkbit` block](#hawkbit-block)) | None                                                                                 |\n</div>\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-ins lm-diff-block\">\n  ```yaml\n  artifacts:\n    storage:\n      type: s3                       # local (default) or s3\n      s3_bucket: \"ota-updates\"\n      s3_prefix: \"ota/artifacts/\"\n      s3_endpoint: \"http://rustfs:9000\"\n      s3_path_style: true\n      s3_access_key_id_file: /etc/ota/s3/access-key\n      s3_secret_access_key_file: /etc/ota/s3/secret-key\n    encryption:\n      type: none                     # none (default), local or awskms\n  ```\n</div>\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-ins lm-diff-block\">\n  Storage (*.storage) [#storage-storage]\n</div>\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-ins lm-diff-block\">\n  | Key                                                  | Default             | Description                                                                                                                                                                                            |\n  | ---------------------------------------------------- | ------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ |\n  | `type`                                               | `local`             | `local` or `s3`.                                                                                                                                                                                       |\n  | `local_dir`                                          | block default above | Directory for `local`. Created with mode `0700`.                                                                                                                                                       |\n  | `s3_bucket`                                          | none                | Required for `s3`. The bucket must exist: the service checks access at start-up and stops if it fails (`S3 bucket \"x\" is not accessible at ...`).                                                      |\n  | `s3_region`                                          | `us-east-1`         | AWS region.                                                                                                                                                                                            |\n  | `s3_endpoint`                                        | empty (AWS)         | URL of an S3-compatible store such as RustFS or MinIO.                                                                                                                                                 |\n  | `s3_path_style`                                      | `false`             | Address buckets as `<endpoint>/<bucket>`. Most self-hosted stores need `true`.                                                                                                                         |\n  | `s3_prefix`                                          | empty               | Keeps every key under a \"folder\" of the bucket. One bucket can hold several stores, hawkBit's artifacts included. A missing trailing `/` is added.                                                     |\n  | `s3_access_key_id`, `s3_secret_access_key`           | none                | Static credentials. Set both or neither.                                                                                                                                                               |\n  | `s3_access_key_id_file`, `s3_secret_access_key_file` | none                | The same credentials read from files, for example a mounted Kubernetes Secret. Re-read every minute, so a rotated Secret needs no restart. Set both or neither, and not together with the inline pair. |\n</div>\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-ins lm-diff-block\">\n  With no credential set, the AWS default chain applies (environment, IRSA, instance profile).\n</div>\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-ins lm-diff-block\">\n  At-rest encryption (*.encryption) [#at-rest-encryption-encryption]\n</div>\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-ins lm-diff-block\">\n  | Key             | Default     | Description                                      |\n  | --------------- | ----------- | ------------------------------------------------ |\n  | `type`          | `none`      | `none`, `local` or `awskms`.                     |\n  | `local_key`     | none        | Required for `local`: a hex-encoded 32-byte key. |\n  | `awskms_key_id` | none        | Required for `awskms`: key ARN or alias.         |\n  | `awskms_region` | `us-east-1` | Region of the AWS KMS key.                       |\n</div>\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-ins lm-diff-block\">\n  An encrypted object is encrypted and decrypted whole, in memory: with at-rest encryption, reading or writing a 10 GiB artifact needs that much memory, and byte ranges are cut from the decrypted copy. A store with encryption enabled cannot use presigned downloads. For large files, prefer encrypting at the store (for example S3 server-side encryption) instead.\n</div>\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-ins lm-diff-block\">\n  Presigned downloads (*.presign) [#presigned-downloads-presign]\n</div>\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-ins lm-diff-block\">\n  With S3 storage, a download can be answered with `302` to a short-lived signed URL of the store instead of streaming the bytes through the service. The caller is authorized first, as for any download. Native mode signs build outputs (`builds.presign`); `hawkbit` mode signs the objects of hawkBit's S3 plugin (`hawkbit.artifact_store.presign`). Artifact downloads by ID in `native` mode are always streamed.\n</div>\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-ins lm-diff-block\">\n  ```yaml\n  builds:\n    storage: {type: s3, s3_bucket: ota-updates, s3_prefix: ota/builds/,\n              s3_endpoint: http://rustfs:9000, s3_path_style: true,\n              s3_access_key_id_file: /etc/ota/s3/access-key,\n              s3_secret_access_key_file: /etc/ota/s3/secret-key}\n    presign:\n      enabled: true\n      public_endpoint: https://updates.example.com\n      ttl: 60s\n      access_key_id_file: /etc/ota/presign/access-key\n      secret_access_key_file: /etc/ota/presign/secret-key\n  ```\n</div>\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-ins lm-diff-block\">\n  | Key                                                            | Default                   | Description                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                           |\n  | -------------------------------------------------------------- | ------------------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n  | `enabled`                                                      | `false`                   | Turns the feature on.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                 |\n  | `public_endpoint`                                              | the storage `s3_endpoint` | Origin of the store **as devices reach it**, without a path (for example the gateway that publishes the bucket). It is what gets signed: the signature covers the `Host` header, so a URL signed for an in-cluster address is refused when a device fetches it through the gateway. Required unless devices reach the store at its own endpoint (for example AWS S3). |\n  | `ttl`                                                          | `60s`                     | How long a URL can **start** a download. Between `1s` and 7 days. A transfer already under way is not cut when it expires.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                            |\n  | `min_remaining`                                                | `min(25s, ttl/2)`         | A URL already signed for the same object is handed out again while it has at least this much validity left, so a retry or resume never gets a URL about to expire. Negative signs a fresh URL on every request. Must be lower than `ttl`.                                                                                                                             |\n  | `access_key_id`, `secret_access_key` (or the `_file` variants) | none                      | The credential that signs. **Required, and it must be a read-only credential different from the storage one.**                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                        |\n</div>\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-ins lm-diff-block\">\n  At start-up the service refuses to run when the signing credential is the storage credential, when it can write to the bucket (it probes with a write), when the store rejects it (it waits up to 2 minutes for the store to accept it), or when `encryption.type` is not `none` (devices would receive ciphertext). If the store cannot be asked at all, it logs a warning and continues. The device must still be able to resolve and reach `public_endpoint`. See [Security](/docs/platform/updates/security#presigned-download-urls).\n</div>\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-ins lm-diff-block\">\n  hawkbit block [#hawkbit-block]\n</div>\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-ins lm-diff-block\">\n  Used only when `mode: hawkbit`.\n</div>\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-ins lm-diff-block\">\n  | Key                                    | Default            | Description                                                                                                                                                                                                                                    |\n  | -------------------------------------- | ------------------ | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n  | `hawkbit.base_url`                     | none (required)    | hawkBit Management API root, for example `http://hawkbit:8080`. Startup fails if it is empty. Calls time out after 30 s; artifact transfers after 6 h.                                                                                         |\n  | `hawkbit.username`, `hawkbit.password` | none               | Management API Basic Auth credentials.                                                                                                                                                                                                         |\n  | `hawkbit.tenant`                       | `DEFAULT`          | DDI tenant path segment.                                                                                                                                                                                                                       |\n  | `hawkbit.ddi_url`                      | `hawkbit.base_url` | hawkBit's DDI root **as devices reach it** (for example `https://updates.example.com/api/hawkbit-ddi`). It is used to build the download URLs devices receive, and its path becomes the prefix of the DDI download route this service serves.  |\n  | `hawkbit.artifact_store`               | none               | Same shape as the [blob storage blocks](#blob-storage-blocks): `storage` must point to the bucket and `s3_prefix` of hawkBit's S3 artifact plugin, and `presign` enables signed downloads. Without it, artifacts are streamed through hawkBit. |\n</div>\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-ins lm-diff-block\">\n  The `storage` (Postgres) block is also required in `hawkbit` mode.\n</div>\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-ins lm-diff-block\">\n  Environment variables [#environment-variables]\n</div>\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-ins lm-diff-block\">\n  Any key of the file can be overridden by an environment variable: the name is the key path in upper case with dots replaced by underscores. `KMS_ENDPOINT` overrides `kms_endpoint`, `STORAGE_PASSWORD` overrides `storage.password` and `BUILDS_PRESIGN_TTL` overrides `builds.presign.ttl`. The key must exist in the file (an empty value is enough): the loader only considers keys it has read from it.\n</div>\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-ins lm-diff-block\">\n  Variables the code reads besides these overrides:\n</div>\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-ins lm-diff-block\">\n  | Variable                    | Description                                                                                                                                              |\n  | --------------------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n  | `OTA_CONFIG_FILE`           | Configuration file path.                                                                                                                                 |\n  | `POD_NAME`, `POD_NAMESPACE` | Identity and namespace for Kubernetes leader election. The Helm chart sets them. If unset, the hostname and the service-account namespace file are used. |\n  | `TMPDIR`                    | Where uploads and builds write temporary files (Go's temporary directory).                                                                               |\n  | `SERVER_PORT`               | Used only by the health check of the Docker image to find the port. Default `10090`.                                                                     |\n  | `AWS_*`                     | The AWS default credential chain, when an S3 or AWS KMS block has no explicit credential.                                                                |\n</div>\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-ins lm-diff-block\">\n  For Docker Compose, the `.env` file is read by `docker-compose.yml`, not by the service: `HOST_IP`, `HAWKBIT_ADMIN_PASSWORD`, `HAWKBIT_ARTIFACT_STORAGE` and the `HAWKBIT_ARTIFACT_S3_*` and `LOCAL_S3_*` variables. See `.env.example` in the repository.\n</div>\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-ins lm-diff-block\">\n  <Callout type=\"warn\" title=\"Debug logging prints the configuration\">\n    With `app.logs.level: debug`, the service writes the whole loaded configuration to the log at start-up, including `storage.password`, `encryption.local_key`, S3 keys and `hawkbit.password`. In `native` mode the `storage` block, password included, is also printed to standard output at every start, whatever the log level. Use `info` or higher anywhere logs are stored or shared, and keep secrets in a secret store, not in the file (see the `*_file` credentials above).\n  </Callout>\n</div>\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-ins lm-diff-block\">\n  Minimal configurations [#minimal-configurations]\n</div>\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-ins lm-diff-block\">\n  `native` mode needs the database, the endpoints it calls and storage paths. Start from `config-docker.yml`:\n</div>\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-ins lm-diff-block\">\n  ```yaml\n  app:\n    logs: {level: info}\n    server: {listen_address: \"0.0.0.0\", port: 10090}\n  storage:\n    provider: postgres\n    hostname: postgres\n    port: 5432\n    username: postgres\n    password: \"<password>\"\n  dms_endpoint: \"http://<lamassu>/api/devmanager\"\n  kms_endpoint: \"http://<lamassu>/api/kms/v1\"\n  wfx_endpoint: \"http://custom-wfx:8081/\"\n  update_domain: \"http://updates:10090/updates/v1/\"\n  symkms_endpoint: \"http://updates:10090\"\n  update_files_base_dir: \"/data/update_files\"\n  sym_keys_dir: \"/data/symmetric_keys\"\n  sign_script_path: \"/app/sign_kms.sh\"\n  workflow_file: \"/app/wfx/workflow/dau/wfx.workflow.dau.direct.yml\"\n  symkms:\n    storage: {type: local, local_dir: \"/data/symmetric_keys\"}\n    encryption: {type: none}\n  ```\n</div>\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-ins lm-diff-block\">\n  `hawkbit` mode needs `mode: hawkbit`, `hawkbit.base_url`, the Management API credentials and the `storage` block. Add the build settings only if you want builds. The keys below are the ones `config-hawkbit.yml` uses:\n</div>\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-ins lm-diff-block\">\n  ```yaml\n  mode: hawkbit\n  hawkbit:\n    base_url: \"http://hawkbit:8080\"\n    username: \"admin\"\n    password: \"<hawkbit admin password>\"\n  swugenerator_path: \"/usr/local/bin/swugenerator\"   # builds .swu modules\n  sign_script_path: \"/app/sign_kms.sh\"               # with kms_endpoint: signing\n  kms_endpoint: \"http://<lamassu>/api/kms/v1\"\n  symkms_endpoint: \"http://localhost:10090\"          # with the symkms block: encryption\n  sym_keys_dir: \"/data/symmetric_keys\"\n  symkms:\n    storage: {type: local, local_dir: \"/data/symmetric_keys\"}\n  ```\n</div>\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-ins lm-diff-block\">\n  In `hawkbit` mode the SymKMS routes are registered only when `symkms.storage.type` or `sym_keys_dir` is set. With `symkms_endpoint` set but neither of those, every key lookup would be answered `404` by the service itself.\n</div>\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-ins lm-diff-block\">\n  SymKMS as its own process [#symkms-as-its-own-process]\n</div>\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-ins lm-diff-block\">\n  The `cmd/symkms` binary runs SymKMS alone. Its configuration has only `app` (as above), `symkms` (a storage block) and `sym_keys_dir`. Its key directory defaults to `/data/symmetric_keys` when neither `symkms.storage.local_dir` nor `sym_keys_dir` is set. It serves `/symkms/v1/*` but no `/health` route, and it has the same lack of authentication as the monolith: see [Security](/docs/platform/updates/security).\n</div>\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-ins lm-diff-block\">\n  Related pages [#related-pages]\n</div>\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-ins lm-diff-block\">\n  * [Operations](/docs/platform/updates/operations) for the Helm values, scaling and backups.\n  * [hawkBit mode](/docs/platform/updates/hawkbit-mode) for what changes in that backend.\n  * [Signing and encryption](/docs/platform/updates/signing-and-encryption) for the keys behind `kms_endpoint` and `symkms`.\n  * [Troubleshooting](/docs/platform/updates/troubleshooting) for start-up errors.\n</div>\n"]
